# PaSTTeL — VMCAI 2027 Artifact

Artifact of the paper **"PaSTTeL: Parallel analysiS framework for Termination and non-Termination of
Lasso programs"** (VMCAI 2027).

PaSTTeL proves termination or non-termination of lasso programs (a stem followed by a loop). It
synthesises ranking functions and non-termination witnesses with an SMT solver (Z3), racing its
techniques on several cores. The paper evaluates it in two ways, both replicated by this artifact:

| | Comparison | Granularity | Paper |
|---|---|---|---|
| **[1]** | **ULR-Baseline** (Ultimate's LassoRanker) vs **P-ULR-Seq** and **P-ULR-Par7** (PaSTTeL on 1 and 7 cores) | per lasso trace: Ultimate extracts the traces of each program, PaSTTeL replays them | Table 1, Figure 5 |
| **[2]** | **Ultimate-LR** vs **Ultimate-PL**: Ultimate Büchi Automizer with LassoRanker, or with PaSTTeL, as its termination backend | per C/Boogie program, end to end | Figure 6 |

ULR-Baseline and Ultimate-LR are both LassoRanker, measured differently: ULR-Baseline is its time on
each lasso trace, Ultimate-LR a whole Ultimate run on each program.

The two comparisons use two releases of Ultimate, which are simply two versions of it:
`tools/UAutomizer-linux` (Ultimate 0.3.1) is the one we ran ULR-Baseline vs P-ULR with, and
`tools/UAutomizer-PaSTTeL-linux` (Ultimate 0.3.2), a later version, integrates PaSTTeL: it runs both
Ultimate-LR and Ultimate-PL. Both ship with their sources (section 6, RQ3).

1. [Artifact contents](#1-artifact-contents)
2. [Getting started](#2-getting-started) — smoke test, about 2 minutes
3. [Replicating the paper](#3-replicating-the-paper) — from our logs in seconds, a subset in about 3 hours, or everything
4. [Reading the results](#4-reading-the-results)
5. [Resource requirements](#5-resource-requirements)
6. [Reusing the artifact](#6-reusing-the-artifact)
7. [Requested badges](#7-requested-badges)

