## 2. Getting started

**Needs:** an x86-64 host with Docker ≥ 20.10, 16 GB of RAM, 8 cores recommended, 20 GB of disk.
No network access is needed with the pre-built image.

The artifact is the archive `pasttel-vmcai27-artifact.tar.gz` on Zenodo. Extract it first: the
Docker image is a file inside it, `pasttel-docker-image-vmcai27.tar.gz`, and every command below runs
from the directory it extracts to.

```bash
# Check the download: next to the archive, the .sha256 file from Zenodo (also given on EasyChair)
sha256sum -c pasttel-vmcai27-artifact.tar.gz.sha256      # macOS: shasum -a 256 -c ...

# Extract it (3.5 GB) and enter its directory
tar -xzf pasttel-vmcai27-artifact.tar.gz
cd pasttel-vmcai27-artifact

# Check, then load the image shipped inside the artifact
sha256sum -c pasttel-docker-image-vmcai27.tar.gz.sha256
docker load --input pasttel-docker-image-vmcai27.tar.gz

# Open a shell in the container; everything written to /app/output appears in ./output on the host.
# Create ./output first: Docker Desktop (macOS, Windows, Linux) may fail to create it itself.
mkdir -p output
docker run -it --rm -v "$(pwd)/output:/app/output" pasttel-artifact:vmcai27

# Inside the container (working directory /app): both comparisons on 10 small programs
bash scripts/run_smoke_test.sh
```

SHA256 of `pasttel-docker-image-vmcai27.tar.gz`: `f5bd05c2c89ec76f7564e2df9561762bb14fe40972e69e818b02494883aac459`

Alternatives:
- With Docker Compose: `mkdir -p output`, `docker compose up -d`, `docker compose exec pasttel bash`,
  and finally `docker compose down`.
- Build the image yourself: `docker build -t pasttel-artifact:vmcai27 .` This takes about 10
  minutes, downloads Ubuntu packages, and runs PaSTTeL's test suite and the smoke test: the build
  fails if either does.
- Without Docker, on Ubuntu 24.04 x86-64: `bash scripts/install.sh` (section 6), then
  `bash scripts/run_smoke_test.sh`.

**Expected outcome.** The smoke test takes about 2 minutes (1 min 50 s in the container on the test
machine, section 5) and exits with status 0. It ends with:

```
  OK      ULR-Baseline vs P-ULR-Seq                        15 row(s)  ulr_vs_pulr/results_P-ULR-Seq_z3.csv
  OK      ULR-Baseline vs P-ULR-Par7                       15 row(s)  ulr_vs_pulr/results_P-ULR-Par7_z3.csv
  OK      Ultimate-LR vs Ultimate-PL (per program)         10 row(s)  ulr_vs_upl/results_ULR_vs_UPL.csv
```

These lines are followed by the two tables of the paper computed on these 10 programs, and by the
paths of the scatter plots (`.html` files that open in any browser, offline). A `FAILED` line means
that comparison produced nothing; the Ultimate and PaSTTeL logs next to its CSV say why. The
complete expected output is `logs/smoke_test_output.log`.

### Smoke test programs and expected verdicts

`benchmarks/smoke_test/full_programs_c_bpl/` holds the 10 programs of the smoke test. Ultimate
extracts 15 lasso traces from them; all tools agree on every one:

| Program | Language | Category | Traces | ULR-Baseline, P-ULR-Seq, P-ULR-Par7 (per trace) | Ultimate-LR, Ultimate-PL (program) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `2Nested-1.c` | C | ALL_INT_VARS | 1 | TERMINATING | terminating |
| `AliasDarteFeautrierGonnord-SAS2010-counterex1b_true-termination.c` | C | BOOLEAN_OP | 6 | #1 infeasible, left out; #2–6 TERMINATING | terminating |
| `java_DivMinus1.c` | C | ALL_INT_VARS | 1 | TERMINATING | terminating |
| `recursive.c` | C | ALL_INT_VARS | 1 | NONTERMINATING | non-terminating |
| `ATVA2013-nonIntegral-real.bpl` | Boogie | REAL_VARS | 1 | NONTERMINATING | non-terminating |
| `Fibonacci.bpl` | Boogie | ALL_INT_VARS | 1 | NONTERMINATING | non-terminating |
| `Lobnya-Boolean.bpl` | Boogie | BOOLEAN_OP | 1 | TERMINATING | terminating |
| `loopWithBreak.bpl` | Boogie | ALL_INT_VARS | 1 | TERMINATING | terminating |
| `LTL_Coolant_Nonterminating01.bpl` | Boogie | ALL_INT_VARS | 1 | NONTERMINATING | non-terminating |
| `NonterminationDifficult05_ComplexEigenvalues.bpl` | Boogie | REAL_VARS | 1 | NONTERMINATING | non-terminating |

- **Category** is the class a trace is sorted into by the types of its variables
  (`pasttel/scripts/split_specific.sh`). P-ULR replays four of them, as in the paper:
  - ALL_INT_VARS: integer variables only;
  - BOOLEAN_OP: at least one Boolean variable;
  - REAL_VARS: at least one real variable;
  - ARRAY_OP: at least one array of integers, reals or Booleans, whatever the other variables.

  The other classes are Ultimate's only, and none occurs here: FUNCT_SIGNATURE (uninterpreted
  functions), UNDEF_TYPE (a variable of another type), UNKNOWN_LOOP (Ultimate could not give the loop's formula) and OTHERS (none of the
  above, e.g. arrays of another element type).
- **The 14 feasible traces** also ship as the JSON PaSTTeL reads, in
  `benchmarks/smoke_test/json_lassos_from_full_programs/`. PaSTTeL analyses them without Ultimate
  in about a second (section 6, Running P-ULR standalone).

---

## 3. Replicating the paper

All commands run inside the container, from `/app`. Every script prints its options with `--help`.

Each result of the paper, and where to check it. "Logs" is our run, rebuilt in seconds (3.1); "rerun"
runs it again, on a subset (3.2) or in full (3.3).

| Paper | Result | Check it with |
|---|---|---|
| Table 1 | ULR-Baseline, P-ULR-Seq, P-ULR-Par7 on 5,727 lasso traces: 4,141 terminating, 1,586 non-terminating, and their times (rounded to the second in the paper, to the hundredth by the scripts) | logs: 3.1 [1]; rerun: `run_full_evaluation.sh --parts pulr` |
| §6, Sequential and Parallel Comparison | P-ULR-Seq gains 1,088 s, and divides the termination time by 11 (1,173 s → 102 s); P-ULR-Par7 gains 1,143 s, and divides the total time by 23 | the totals of Table 1: 1,087.86 s and ×11.5, 1,142.79 s and ×23.8 |
| §6 | 80 % of the lassos admit an affine ranking function | the command below |
| Figure 5 | P-ULR-Seq against ULR-Baseline | `logs/results_P-ULR-Seq_z3_scatter.html`, rebuilt by 3.1 [1] |
| §6, Integrate P-ULR inside Ultimate; Figure 6 | Ultimate-LR vs Ultimate-PL on 1,087 programs: 883 solved by both, 13,491 s → 12,763 s (−5.4 %; 13,490.75 s and 12,762.92 s in the table), 28 solved by Ultimate-PL only, 8 by Ultimate-LR only | logs: 3.1 [2] and `logs/results_ULR_vs_UPL_wall.html`; rerun: `run_full_evaluation.sh --parts upl` |
| §6 | the Ultimate plugin, 520 lines of code | `ultimate/` (section 6, PaSTTeL inside Ultimate) |
| Figures 1 and 4, §5 | the example lasso and its ranking function | section 6, Running P-ULR standalone |
| §3–§5 | Z3 and CVC5, the three linearization modes, the five templates, fixpoint and GNTA, the library, the single interface of a technique | section 6 |

```bash
# §6: the lassos ULR-Baseline proves terminating, and how many of them with the affine template
python3 -c "import csv; t = [r for r in csv.DictReader(open('logs/results_P-ULR-Seq_z3.csv')) if r['Result Code'] == 'TERMINATING']; a = sum(r['Algo'].startswith('Affine') for r in t); print(a, 'of', len(t), f'({100 * a / len(t):.0f}%)')"
# prints: 3425 of 4269 (80%)
```

Not replicable exactly, and why (details in 3.4): the times, which depend on the machine; the
technique that wins each P-ULR-Par7 race; and, on a few long-running programs, the lasso traces
Ultimate extracts. The conclusions the paper draws from them should hold: P-ULR-Seq and P-ULR-Par7
prove the same traces as ULR-Baseline, much faster; Ultimate-PL solves more programs than
Ultimate-LR, in less cumulative time on the programs both solve.

### 3.1 From our logs, in seconds

`logs/` holds our own runs. Its scatter plots open directly in a browser:
`results_P-ULR-Seq_z3_scatter.html` (Figure 5), `results_P-ULR-Par7_z3_scatter.html` and
`results_ULR_vs_UPL_wall.html` (Figure 6). The analysis scripts rebuild the paper's tables and these
plots from our CSVs, without running Ultimate:

```bash
mkdir -p output/paper
cp logs/results_*.csv output/paper/

# [1] ULR-Baseline vs P-ULR: Table 1 and the two scatter plots, from our per-trace CSVs
python3 pasttel/scripts/benchmark_ultimate_vs_pasttel.py --baseline-name UAutomizer-linux --log \
    --table output/paper/results_P-ULR-Seq_z3.csv output/paper/results_P-ULR-Par7_z3.csv

# [2] Ultimate-LR vs Ultimate-PL: the table and the scatter plot, from our per-program CSV
python3 pasttel/scripts/benchmark_ulr_vs_upl.py --plot output/paper/results_ULR_vs_UPL.csv \
    --timeout 1000 --log-scale
```

They print the paper's Table 1 and the figures of its Section 6, shown in
[Section 4](#4-reading-the-results).

The two zips of `logs/` go one step further back, to the raw data these CSVs come from (1.5 GB each,
unpacked), still without running Ultimate:

- [1] PaSTTeL re-analyses the paper's 5727 lasso traces itself. The ULR-Baseline column then comes
  from our Ultimate run, and the P-ULR columns from your machine. This takes about 20 minutes for
  P-ULR-Seq and 17 for P-ULR-Par7.
- [2] The script parses Ultimate's own logs, one per program and side, back into the per-program
  CSV, in a few seconds: the same rows as `logs/results_ULR_vs_UPL.csv`.

```bash
# [1] The lasso traces of our run, sorted by category, each with the JSON PaSTTeL read
unzip -q logs/ULR_vs_PULR_logs.zip -d output/paper
bash scripts/run_ulr_vs_pulr.sh --only seq,par --lassos output/paper/ULR_vs_PULR_logs/lasso_traces \
    --output output/replay

# [2] Ultimate's logs of our run: the CSV, the table and the scatter plot, rebuilt from them
unzip -q logs/ULR_vs_UPL_logs.zip -d output/paper
python3 pasttel/scripts/benchmark_ulr_vs_upl.py --log-dir output/paper/ULR_vs_UPL_logs --timeout 1000 \
    --log-scale --output output/raw/results_ULR_vs_UPL.csv
diff --strip-trailing-cr output/raw/results_ULR_vs_UPL.csv logs/results_ULR_vs_UPL.csv   # prints nothing
```

### 3.2 Subset: 360 + 100 programs, about 3 hours

```bash
bash scripts/run_full_evaluation.sh --subset                # both comparisons, results in output/subset/
bash scripts/run_full_evaluation.sh --subset --parts pulr   # [1] only, about 1 h 30 min
bash scripts/run_full_evaluation.sh --subset --parts upl    # [2] only, about 1 h 40 min
```

Each comparison runs on programs of its own benchmark, chosen so that they reproduce its
results (`benchmarks/subset/select_subsets.py`, from our logs):
- [1] on the 360 programs of `benchmarks/subset/ulr_vs_pulr` (200 C, 160 Boogie): within 15 % of the
  speed-ups of Table 1 (×12.1 and ×27.8 on terminating traces, ×6.9 and ×6.4 on non-terminating ones
  in our logs),
  with no single trace above 15 % of a total;
- [2] on the 100 programs of `benchmarks/subset/ulr_vs_upl` (98 C, 2 Boogie): the benchmark's classes in its
  proportions (solved by both runs, by Ultimate-PL only, by Ultimate-LR only, by neither), and its
  time differences (−11.3 % terminating, +2.8 % non-terminating, −7.4 % in total in our logs).

A uniform random draw does not reproduce them: a few traces in [1], and a few long programs in [2],
carry most of the differences. Each README gives the method, the seed found, the bounds and our
result for every program.

[1] caps each Ultimate run at 300 s instead of 3,000 s; its programs are among those our run
extracted within 200 s, so they yield the same traces. [2] keeps the paper's 1,000 s: the gain of
Ultimate-PL comes from long runs. PaSTTeL keeps the paper's timeouts. The times above come from our
logs of these very programs.

### 3.3 Full evaluation: about 5.5 days

```bash
bash scripts/run_full_evaluation.sh                 # both comparisons, results in output/full/
bash scripts/run_full_evaluation.sh --parts pulr    # [1] only: benchmarks/ulr_vs_pulr, 2,856 programs
bash scripts/run_full_evaluation.sh --parts upl     # [2] only: benchmarks/ulr_vs_upl, 1,087 programs
```

| Option | Default (the paper's) | |
|---|---|---|
| `--ultimate-timeout` | 3000 s | per Ultimate run in [1] |
| `--upl-timeout` | 1000 s | per Ultimate run in [2], each side |
| `--input`, `--output` | see above | other programs, other output directory |

PaSTTeL always keeps the paper's settings: 600 s per trace in [1], on 1 and 7 cores; 20 s per lasso
on 5 cores inside Ultimate-PL, past which LassoRanker takes over. Ultimate dominates the running time. The
banner printed at start-up gives the worst case, in which every run hits its timeout.

### 3.4 What may differ from our numbers

- **Times** depend on the machine and its load. Run one experiment at a time on an idle machine;
  the scripts are sequential on purpose. Compare verdict counts and time totals, not individual
  times.
- **The lasso traces Ultimate extracts** are identical from run to run on most programs, but not
  on some long-running ones. We traced this to Java identity hash codes, which change between
  runs; no Ultimate setting controls it. [1] stays exact, since ULR-Baseline and P-ULR are timed
  on the very same trace, but the number of traces can vary. In [2], the two runs of a program
  are independent, so the lassos they analyse can differ.
- **P-ULR-Par7** races its techniques: the winning technique and its time can change from run to
  run.
- **CVC5** is installed for PaSTTeL's test suite, which runs every test with both solvers, and for
  anyone who wants to try it (`pasttel -s cvc5`). The experiments use Z3 only: `run_ulr_vs_pulr.sh`
  runs PaSTTeL with `--solver z3`, and Ultimate-PL with PaSTTeL's default solver, Z3.

---

## 4. Reading the results

Each comparison writes a `summary_tables.log`, which the script also prints: the paper's table,
then the paths of the scatter plots and CSVs. An instance counts as solved by a tool that proved
it `TERMINATING` or `NONTERMINATING`; timeouts and UNKNOWN answers solve nothing and add no
time. `*` marks the best value, set in bold in the paper. **VBS**, the virtual best solver,
solves every instance that any tool solved, each at the best tool's time.

### [1] ULR-Baseline vs P-ULR (`ulr_vs_pulr/`)

On our run:

```
ULR-Baseline vs P-ULR, ULR-Baseline from UAutomizer-linux
5727 conclusive anlysis lasso programs (others are left out: infeasible or unchecked by Ultimate)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
Tool          #Terminating    Time (s)   #Non-Terminating    Time (s)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
VBS                   4269      102.54                1586        2.81
ULR-Baseline          4141     1172.99                1586       19.90
P-ULR-Seq             4141      101.92                1586        3.11*
P-ULR-Par7            4141       46.77*               1586        3.33
----------------------------------------------------------------------
Tools: the 5727 traces every tool proved. VBS: the traces any tool proved, each at its best time. * best.
Not proved: ULR-Baseline 17 (UNKNOWN 17) | P-ULR-Seq 145 (UNKNOWN 128, not run 17) | P-ULR-Par7 145 (UNKNOWN 128, not run 17)
Contradictions: none
```

- **Tool rows** count the traces that every tool proved, and add up each tool's time on exactly
  these traces.
- **Infeasible and unchecked traces** are not termination problems and are left out.
- **Not proved** gives, for each tool, the traces it did not prove and why. PaSTTeL is not run on
  the traces ULR-Baseline left UNKNOWN.

There is one scatter plot per P-ULR configuration: `results_P-ULR-Seq_z3_scatter.html` is Figure 5,
and `results_P-ULR-Par7_z3_scatter.html` the same for P-ULR-Par7, which is not in the paper. As in
Figure 5, each has two panels, the traces both tools proved terminating (left, green) and
non-terminating (right, blue), one + per trace, ULR-Baseline time on the X axis and P-ULR time on
the Y axis; a contradiction would be a black star. Hovering a point gives each tool's verdict, the
strategy that proved it (a ranking-function template, Fixpoint or GNTA) and its time. The directory
also holds the per-trace CSVs, `lasso_traces/` with the dumped traces (by category, each
`lasso_trace_<N>.txt` with the `lasso_trace_<N>.json` PaSTTeL reads) and Ultimate's log of each
program, and `pasttel_logs/` with PaSTTeL's.

### [2] Ultimate-LR vs Ultimate-PL (`ulr_vs_upl/`)

On our run:

```
Ultimate-LR vs Ultimate-PL on 1087 C/BPL programs, Ultimate timeout 1000 s per run, wall clock
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Tool          #Solved   #Terminating    Time (s)   #Non-Terminating    Time (s)    Total (s)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
VBS               919             591     8242.93                 328     3853.70      12096.63
Ultimate-LR       891             558     9442.55                 325     4048.20*     13490.75
Ultimate-PL       911*            558     8511.54*                325     4251.39      12762.92*
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Ultimate-LR, Ultimate-PL: #Solved by each run; times on the 883 programs both runs solved,
  Total being the Cumulative Intersection Time. VBS: the programs either run solved, each at its
  better time. * best.
Not solved: Ultimate-LR 196 (UNKNOWN 152, TIMEOUT 44) | Ultimate-PL 176 (UNKNOWN 140, TIMEOUT 36)
Solved by one run only: Ultimate-LR 8, Ultimate-PL 28
PaSTTeL inside Ultimate-PL: 2284 calls, 2152 conclusive, 132 without result (LassoRanker took over), 10 certificates not mapped back
Contradictions: none
```

- **#Solved** is each run's own count.
- **The time columns** are wall clock on the programs both runs solved, split by verdict.
  **Total** is the paper's Cumulative Intersection Time.
- **During the run**, each program prints one line per side: its wall clock and its verdict. The
  verdict is `proved terminating`, `proved nonterminating`, `TIMEOUT` (stopped at `--timeout`) or
  `UNKNOWN` (Ultimate gave up, or stopped earlier without a verdict, e.g. on an exception).
- **The scatter plot** `results_ULR_vs_UPL_wall.html` is Figure 6: terminating programs on the left
  (green), non-terminating ones on the right (blue). A +: both runs solved the program. A triangle:
  only one run did; the other answered `TIMEOUT` or `UNKNOWN` and is drawn beyond the red dashed
  timeout line on its axis (right: Ultimate-LR, top: Ultimate-PL). Programs neither run solved are
  not drawn.
- **`logs/`** (in our run: `logs/ULR_vs_UPL_logs.zip`) keeps, for each program and side, Ultimate's
  log (`.log`), the wall clock (`.wall_ms`) and the exit status (`.exit`, 124 meaning stopped at the
  time limit).

Ultimate-LR and Ultimate-PL are the same Ultimate release (`tools/UAutomizer-PaSTTeL-linux`), and
they differ only in the termination analysis backend:
- **Ultimate-LR** runs with Ultimate's default settings, exactly like the trace extraction of [1].
- **Ultimate-PL** adds `tools/settings/BuchiAutomizerPasttel.epf.in`, whose only lines select
  PaSTTeL and set its binary, timeout and cores.
- **Fallback:** when PaSTTeL does not prove a lasso terminating, Ultimate-PL falls back to LassoRanker's
  templates.

### CSV columns

- **Per trace** (`results_P-ULR-*_z3.csv`):
  - `Result Code` is ULR-Baseline's verdict, and `ULR-Baseline (ms)` its time: fixpoint check +
    termination + non-termination.
  - `P-ULR-Seq` / `P-ULR-Par7` is PaSTTeL's analysis time, `PaSTTeL-TOTAL` its process time, and
    `PaSTTeL Status` its verdict.
  - `Stem/Loop/Total Size Trace` give the size of the trace, and `Algo` the winning technique of
    each tool.
- **Per program** (`results_ULR_vs_UPL.csv`):
  - `Ultimate-LR/Ultimate-PL Verdict` is as above; `NO LOG` means that side was not run.
  - `Ultimate-LR/Ultimate-PL Wall (ms)` is measured by the script. `Plugin (ms)` and `Lassos (ms)` come from
    Ultimate's statistics; `benchmark_ulr_vs_upl.py --plot <csv> --col plugin|lassos` plots them.
  - `Ultimate-PL PaSTTeL Calls / Success / No Result / Unmapped` describe PaSTTeL's activity inside
    Ultimate-PL.
  - `Agreement` and `Speedup (wall)` are also given; the speedup only when both runs solved the
    program.

### Checking the verdicts

What shows that the verdicts behind the tables are right:
- **Two independent tools agree.** Every table ends with a `Contradictions` line: none on the
  5,727 traces both ULR-Baseline and P-ULR prove, and none on the 883 programs both Ultimate-LR and
  Ultimate-PL solve. A contradiction would be listed, and drawn as a black star.
- **PaSTTeL's certificates are checked, then used.** `pasttel -val` re-checks each ranking function
  it synthesises with the SMT solver, and Ultimate-PL always calls PaSTTeL with `-val`. The
  certificate then becomes Ultimate's own termination argument (`PasttelResultMapper`), from which
  Ultimate builds the proof of the whole program. One that does not translate is discarded and LassoRanker takes over: 10 in
  our run, counted on the `PaSTTeL inside Ultimate-PL` line.
- **PaSTTeL's test suite** (247 tests, about 4 minutes): lassos with a known verdict, on both
  solvers, part of them with `-val` or with one template only, plus checks of the supporting
  invariants; the regression tests name the bug each guards against. The image is built only if
  they all pass; to run them again:
  ```bash
  cd /app/pasttel && python3 scripts/test_non_regression.py
  ```
- **Guards in the scripts.** `run_ulr_vs_upl.sh` stops if PaSTTeL cannot be launched, if the
  Ultimate release lacks the PaSTTeL backend, or if Ultimate analyses lassos with other settings
  than ULR-Baseline's (partitioning off, linear synthesis): each would make Ultimate-PL silently
  measure Ultimate-LR.

---

## 5. Resource requirements

| Run | Time | |
|---|---|---|
| `docker build` (optional) or `scripts/install.sh` | about 10 min, or about 3 min | measured on the test machine below |
| `scripts/run_smoke_test.sh` | about 2 min | measured on the test machine below, in the container |
| PaSTTeL's test suite (section 4) | about 4 min | measured on the test machine below |
| From our logs (3.1) | seconds; about 40 min to replay the traces | |
| `run_full_evaluation.sh --subset` | about 3 h | estimated from our logs |
| `run_full_evaluation.sh` | about 5.5 days: 86 h for [1], 45 h for [2] | from our logs |

- **RAM:** 16 GB. Ultimate's JVM may grow up to 12 GB (`-Xmx12G` in each release's `Ultimate.ini`).
- **Cores:** 8 recommended. P-ULR-Par7 uses 7 cores and Ultimate-PL 5. With fewer cores the scripts still
  run, but no longer in the paper's configuration.
- **Architecture:** x86-64 only; the Ultimate releases and the solvers are x86-64 binaries. On an ARM
  host (e.g. Apple Silicon), Docker emulates x86-64: the smoke test still runs, but several times
  slower, and the times are no longer comparable with ours.
- **Machines.** Our results (section 4) come from the paper's machine; every time given for the
  artifact itself (smoke test, build, test suite) was measured on the test machine.

  | | Paper's machine | Test machine |
  |---|---|---|
  | Kind | virtual machine | physical machine |
  | OS | Ubuntu 24.04, x86-64 | Ubuntu 24.04.5 LTS, x86-64, Linux 7.0 |
  | CPU | Intel Core i5-14400T, 16 vCPUs: 6 performance and 4 efficient cores | AMD Ryzen 7 PRO 6850H: 8 cores, 16 threads |
  | Frequency | up to 4.5 GHz (performance cores; base 1.5 GHz, efficient cores 1.1–3.2 GHz, Intel's specification) | 3.2 GHz base, up to 4.7 GHz (4,787 MHz) |
  | Cache | 20 MB (Intel's specification) | L2 4 MiB, L3 16 MiB |
  | RAM | 30 GiB | 30.6 GiB, and 8 GiB of swap |
  | Disk | – | NVMe SSD |
  | Software | Z3 4.16.0 | Docker 27.1.1; in the image: Java 21, Z3 4.16.0, CVC5 1.3.4 |
- **Timeouts:** the paper's timeouts are the scripts' defaults, all defined in `scripts/common.sh`.

